## Seam notes

**S1 — `client/src/adapter/types.ts` (4499 lines, high-collision shared file; phase 1).** Append only, as a `// ── Tournament ──` banner section after the existing lobby section. Never reorder or reformat existing exports. Re-read before editing if the file changed.

**S2 — `PairingView` name collision (frozen in phase 1, propagates to 4 and 5).** `adapter/draft-adapter.ts` already exports `PairingView` with an incompatible shape, imported by `EliminationBracket.tsx`, `multiplayerDraftStore.ts`, `p2p-draft-host.ts`, `draftPodHostAdapter.ts` and three adapter tests. Phase 1 must pick a distinct name for the broker mirror — `TournamentPairingView` is the natural choice — and phases 4/5 must use it. Do not rename or touch the draft-side type; that is other, shipped work.

**S3 — `SubscribeLobby` refcount is shared; the fix is phase 2's, but it is only *possible* because of a constraint on phase 1 (phase 2, with a binding constraint on phase 1).**

`subscribeLobbyOver`'s returned detach sends `UnsubscribeLobby` unconditionally (`brokerClient.ts:656`, with the matching `SubscribeLobby` at `:650`), and the store fires it when `lobbySubscribers.size === 0`. Broker-side, `AddSubscriber`/`RemoveSubscriber` insert and remove *this connection's `tx`* from one set (`crates/lobby-broker/src/broker.rs:317-332` for the add, `:339-342` for the remove) — a single removal kills the subscription no matter how many `SubscribeLobby` frames preceded it. So the draft's "tournament subscribe sends `SubscribeLobby` but explicitly not `UnsubscribeLobby`" is a half-measure that leaks in the other direction: the last lobby subscriber leaving silently kills every live tournament subscription on the same socket.

Phase 2 must introduce **one** `SubscribeLobby` refcount spanning both subscriber sets, with `UnsubscribeLobby` sent only when the combined count reaches zero. This is a required correction to the draft, not an optional refinement, and it carries its own discriminating tests (listed in phase 2).

**Both directions are tested at phase 2's boundary, not one.** The detach direction (last lobby subscriber must not unsubscribe while a tournament subscriber lives) is the direction the draft got wrong. The attach direction is the direction a *unification* can get wrong: `SubscribeLobby` → `Outbound::AddSubscriber` is the only path into the delivery set, so a refcount that treats a tournament subscriber as "already covered" when no lobby subscriber exists produces a page that renders and never updates — a failure that, without phase 2's attach-side bullet, would first surface in phase 5 as a mysterious dead subscription. Its negative sibling (a *second* tournament subscriber must not re-send) is what proves the count is shared rather than per-subscriber.

**Why this constrains phase 1.** Phase 2's scope is `multiplayerStore.ts` alone; phase 1's `tournamentClient.ts` is frozen by the time phase 2 runs. The natural thing for a stateless executor to do in phase 1 is mirror the established `subscribeLobbyOver` precedent and have `subscribeTournamentsOver` send `SubscribeLobby` on attach and `UnsubscribeLobby` on detach. If it does, the frames live outside the refcount the store owns, and phase 2 **cannot** unify them without editing a file that is not in its scope — the seam's own mandate becomes unenforceable at the boundary it is assigned to. Phase 1 therefore carries an explicit, tested constraint: `subscribeTournamentsOver` sends those frames **zero times** and handles inbound `TournamentListUpdate` / `TournamentUpdate` / `TournamentRemoved` only. It is guarded by phase 1's static source-text assertion plus a zero-`send` attach/detach test with a positive reach-guard. Treat that assertion as load-bearing for S3, not as style policing: deleting it re-opens this seam.

**The guard must not forbid the explanation.** S3 positively wants `tournamentClient.ts` to carry a doc comment saying why these frames are deliberately absent and where they live instead — `brokerClient.ts:595` sets the precedent by naming `UnsubscribeLobby` inside exactly such a comment. That is why phase 1's assertion is scoped to `send(` call arguments rather than to the raw file text: a whole-file `toContain` check would make the seam's own documentation illegal. See phase 1's verification plan for the two acceptable implementations and the required positive control.

**S4 — locale catalogs are shared across phases 3, 4 and 5, and are gated by *two* auto-discovering tests plus one now-parameterized pinned test.**

Phase 3 authors the initial key set but does **not** freeze it: phases 4 and 5 will each discover keys they need. Every key added in a later phase must land in all 7 `tournament.json` files (and `menu.json` for nav) in that same commit. Treat these 14 files as a shared surface across three phases.

The contract every such addition must satisfy is **three-part**, not just flat key parity:

1. **Flat key parity** — `client/src/i18n/resources.test.ts` (auto-discovering) *and* `localeParity.test.ts:272` (auto-discovering). The latter is exact **in both directions**: `:276` rejects a key present in English and missing from a locale; `:279` rejects a key present in a locale and absent from English. A key added to `en/` and forgotten in one mirror goes red in both tests.
2. **Interpolation-placeholder parity** — `localeParity.test.ts:282`, auto-discovering over `readdirSync`'d locales × namespaces. Every `{{name}}` in a translated value must match the English value's placeholder set exactly. This binds phase 5's `t("errors.serverRejected", { message })` and every count-bearing or code-bearing string phases 4 and 5 add. `KNOWN_PLACEHOLDER_GAPS` (`:32`) is an allowlist — **do not add tournament entries to it**; `:303` fails on any entry that is no longer needed, so an unnecessary exemption is self-reporting.
3. **Four-form plural families — authored in all 7 catalogs including English, and enforced through a namespace-parameterized `FOUR_FORM_STEMS`.**

   **This is not a "Polish only" requirement.** *(Corrected, revision 3 finding B — this is the gap most likely to actually bite an implementer, so read it before authoring any count-bearing key.)* Polish is the locale whose grammar *needs* four forms (`_one` / `_few` / `_many` / `_other`; `localeParity.test.ts:266-268` pins i18next's Polish suffix resolution as `1→_one`, `2→_few`, `5→_many`, `12→_many`, `1.5→_other`). But i18next resolves plurals by looking up *suffixed keys*, and part 1's key parity is exact in **both** directions. So the moment English authors `entrants_one` / `entrants_few` / `entrants_many` / `entrants_other`, **every one of the 7 catalogs — English included — must carry all four suffixed keys**, even though English, French, German, Spanish, Italian and Portuguese only grammatically distinguish two.

   This is the existing house convention, not an invention. `en/draft.json:219-222` carries `workspace.count.deck_{one,few,many,other}` with `_few`, `_many` and `_other` all reading "Deck ({{count}} cards)"; `fr/draft.json:220-221` mirrors it the same way. Redundant-looking duplicate values in the two-form locales are **correct and required**.

   **Author all four suffixes in all 7 files, in the same commit, always.** The failure mode this exists to prevent: authoring only `_one`/`_other` in English while Polish carries all four. That goes red as an *extra-key* violation on `pl` (`:279`), and the tempting quick "fix" — deleting Polish's `_few`/`_many` — turns a red test into silently broken Polish grammar. The fix is always to add the missing forms to English and the other five locales, never to remove them from Polish.

   **Enforcement mechanism — the v2 charter's instruction here was wrong and is replaced.** v2 said a phase wanting four-form enforcement for a tournament stem "adds that stem to `FOUR_FORM_STEMS`." Verified against the file: `FOUR_FORM_STEMS` (`:137-154`) has **exactly one use site**, `:202`, inside a loop whose `target` is `load(locale, "draft.json")` (`:201`) — hardcoded, for every locale, independent of which stem is being checked. Appending `"entrants"` would make the test look for `entrants_one` … `entrants_other` **inside `draft.json`**, where they can never be. That is 7 locales (`[SOURCE, ...locales]`, `:200`) × 4 suffixes = **28 guaranteed failures**, not zero. Do not follow the v2 instruction.

   **Remediation chosen: (a), parameterize the stem list by namespace.** Phase 3 changes `FOUR_FORM_STEMS` from `readonly string[]` to `ReadonlyArray<{ ns: string; stem: string }>`, keeps all 16 existing entries with `ns: "draft.json"`, and changes `:201` to `load(locale, entry.ns)`. Phases 3, 4 and 5 then append `{ ns: "tournament.json", stem: "<stem>" }` for each count-bearing tournament key they author.

   **Reasoning (finding A asked for this explicitly).** The alternative — declaring four forms a convention-only requirement with no mechanical gate — leaves one real hole that part 1 does *not* close. Key parity is English-driven: it forces every locale to match English, but nothing forces **English itself** to carry `_few`/`_many`. An author who writes only `entrants_one`/`entrants_other` in `en/tournament.json` and dutifully mirrors exactly those two into `pl/tournament.json` gets a fully green tree and broken Polish plurals in production — precisely the failure this seam exists to prevent, and precisely the one a convention-only rule cannot catch. `FOUR_FORM_STEMS` closes it because its loop runs over `[SOURCE, ...locales]`, pinning English too.

   Against that, the risk of route (a) is low and bounded. All 16 existing entries keep `ns: "draft.json"`, so the loop performs identical loads over identical stems with identical suffixes — the draft assertion is bit-identical before and after, and cannot be destabilized by a change that alters none of its inputs. The refactor touches four lines and no assertion semantics. Neither of the file's other draft-pinned surfaces is touched: `WORKSPACE_SHELL_KEYS` (`:69-135`, pinned at `:168`) is a different constant with a different purpose, and `resolves_polish_one_few_many_and_other_without_fallback` (`:210-268`) asserts literal Polish strings from `pl/draft.json` and is draft-specific by design. Phase 3 carries a positive reach-guard for the refactor itself (flip one entry's `ns` and exactly that entry's four assertions must fail), so a parameterization that silently ignores `entry.ns` cannot pass.

   **Scope-path consequence, stated explicitly.** `client/src/i18n/__tests__/localeParity.test.ts` is added to the scope-path hints of phases 3, 4 and 5 — the executor must know it is allowed to touch it. It is a **test file**, which the T2 counting rule excludes outright, so **no phase's unit count or source scope-path count changes and no phase's fit verdict moves**: phase 3 stays at 5, phase 4 at 6, phase 5 at 5 (+1 conditional). The phase-fit table below is restated unchanged.

Neither `resources.test.ts` nor `localeParity.test.ts` needs editing for parts 1 and 2 in any phase; part 3 is the sole reason any phase touches `localeParity.test.ts`.

